<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ponary massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Paneriai massacre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ponary massacre was the mass murder of about 100,000 people, some 70,000 of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, by German SS units and Lithuanian collaborators at Ponary (Paneriai) near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> between 1941 and 1944. Victims were shot in pits in the forest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Soviet prisoners were also killed there. It was one of the largest killing sites of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Ponary, now Paneriai, is a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. After the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in September 1939 the region was occupied and, on 10 October, transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> under the Soviet-Lithuanian Mutual Assistance Treaty. Following the Soviet annexation of Lithuania in June 1940, work began near Ponary on an oil storage facility for a future military airfield. It was never completed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> took the area in June 1941 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a>. The killing squads used the six large pits dug for the storage tanks to hide the bodies of those they killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The killings began as soon as SS Einsatzkommando 9 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> on 2 July 1941. Most were carried out by the Ypatingasis burys, a Lithuanian volunteer unit eighty men strong. The Vilna Ghetto was established in September, and that month 3,700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were shot in one operation and 6,000 in another, after being rounded up in the city and walked to Paneriai. Most victims were stripped before being shot. By the end of 1941 about 50,000 to 60,000 Vilna Jews had been killed, though estimates for this period differ widely. The pace slowed in 1942, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> took on the ghettoised Jewish workers as slave labour.</p><p>As Soviet troops advanced in 1943, the Nazi units tried to hide the crime under the directive known as Aktion 1005. Eighty prisoners from the Stutthof concentration camp were forced to dig up the bodies and burn them. On 19 April 1944 the prisoners escaped through a tunnel dug with spoons, and the eleven who survived the war helped reveal the massacre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The total number of victims is put at up to 100,000, of whom about 70,000 were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Among the others were Polish intellectuals, including the professor Kazimierz Pelczar and the priest Romuald Świrkowski, members of the Armia Krajowa resistance, Soviet prisoners of war and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani people</a>. Under Soviet rule the official line was that the site held only Soviet citizens. The Polish journalist Kazimierz Sakowicz recorded the killings in a diary kept in hiding, which Rahel Margolis found and published in 1999. The site has monuments to Holocaust victims, erected in 1991, and to Polish victims, erected in 1990 and rebuilt in 2000, and a small museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ponary massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Holocaust was carried out on a vast scale: Jews were murdered at Rumbula near Riga, <b>Ponary</b> near Vilnius, the Ninth Fort in Kaunas and Maly Trostenets near Minsk.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-ostland">Reichskommissariat Ostland</a></li></ul></section>
